core_rev_volatility":0.0,"_gate_margin_vs_hist":null,"_gp_margin_vs_hist":null,"_score_margin_vs_hist":null,"_gate_piotroski":6,"_gp_piotroski":false,"_score_piotroski":66.7,"_gate_spread":null,"_gp_spread":null,"_score_spread":null,"_gate_roic_consistency":null,"_gp_roic_consistency":null,"_score_roic_consistency":null,"_gate_incr_roic":null,"_gp_incr_roic":null,"_score_incr_roic":null,"_gate_margin_advantage":null,"_gp_margin_advantage":null,"_score_margin_advantage":null,"_gate_pool_share":-0.2284139329247581,"_gp_pool_share":false,"_score_pool_share":0.0,"_gate_rev_durability":null,"_gp_rev_durability":null,"_score_rev_durability":0.0,"_gate_fcf_durability":null,"_gp_fcf_durability":null,"_score_fcf_durability":null,"_gate_margins":null,"_gp_margins":null,"_score_margins":null,"_gate_fund_growth":null,"_gp_fund_growth":null,"_score_fund_growth":null,"_gate_shrhldr_yield":0.020451546672520516,"_gp_shrhldr_yield":true,"_score_shrhldr_yield":33.8,"_gate_share_shrink":null,"_gp_share_shrink":null,"_score_share_shrink":0.0,"_gate_sbc_dilution":0.008413423161659057,"_gp_sbc_dilution":true,"_score_sbc_dilution":86.0,"_gate_insider_own":0.12408,"_gp_insider_own":true,"_score_insider_own":82.7,"_gate_insider_buying":1.0,"_gp_insider_buying":true,"_score_insider_buying":100.0,"pf":[],"description_full":"John Marshall Bancorp, Inc. operates as the bank holding company for John Marshall Bank that provides banking products and financial services in Arlington, Fairfax, Loudoun, Prince William, Montgomery, District of Columbia. The company offers checking, NOW, money market accounts, and savings accounts; certificates of deposit; and time, reciprocal IntraFi Demand, IntraFi Money Market, and IntraFi CD deposits. It also offers commercial real estate loans, residential and commercial construction and development loans, commercial and industrial loans, and residential mortgage loans; commercial term loans and lines of credit; U.S. small business administration; mortgage loan; and other loans, such as financing of equipment, receivables, contract administration expenses, and automobile financing. In addition, the company provides debit and credit cards; treasury and cash management services; business and personal insurance; and online and mobile banking with bill pay, sweep accounts, wire transfer, and check imaging and remote deposit capture services; and U.S. treasury bonds, government agency securities, and mortgage-backed securities. It serves small to medium-sized businesses, their owners and employees, professional corporations, non-profits, and individuals. The company was formerly known as John Marshall Bank and changed its name to John Marshall Bancorp, Inc. in March 2017. John Marshall Bancorp, Inc. was founded in 2005 and is headquartered in Reston, Virginia.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Christopher W. Bergstrom | President, CEO & Director | Age 65 | Compensation: $1.4M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 138 people. Revenue per employee of $454K ranks in the below the sector median among Financial Services peers. Each employee generates $160K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $4K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 3\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags elevated compensation governance risk (10/10).","financial_summary":["John Marshall Bancorp, Inc. is a small-cap financial services business with a $327M market cap on $63M of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at -5% over the past three years, and a shrinking top line is the hardest headwind any business can overcome. Operating margins are contracting by -3.9pp, the first warning that competitive pressure is eroding the franchise.","Returns on capital run at 9% against a 8% cost of capital, which means the business barely earns its keep economically.","The business throws off $22M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 35 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.06\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, the balance sheet carries net cash, which is the gold standard: the business never has to make desperate decisions in a bad year, and thin 0.5\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield of 2.0% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insiders hold 12% of the shares, so management thinks like owners because it is one \u2014 the strongest alignment there is, and within its sector the business captures 1.7\u00d7 its fair share of profits, a structural advantage visible in the numbers."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Operating margins contracting -3.9pp YoY \u2014 competitive pressure is eroding the franchise","sev":"red","cat":"trend"},{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (0.3) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Revenue growth in the bottom decile of Financial Services peers \u2014 losing share inside the profit pool","sev":"red","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Credit cycles and regulatory capital \u2014 losses accumulate invisibly in good years and appear all at once, and the time to worry is when underwriting standards are loosest.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Fintech disintermediation \u2014 neobanks, embedded finance, and blockchain rails are unbundling the fee streams that traditional banks took for granted.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Interest rate regime shifts \u2014 a rapid move in rates can invert the yield curve, crush bond portfolios, and trigger deposit flight simultaneously.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Operating margins 15% above sector median \u2014 the quantitative trace of pricing power","Trading at a 53% margin of safety to estimated fair value \u2014 a real cushion against errors in the estimate","Market is pricing in lower growth than the company has historically delivered","Insiders are net buyers (100% buy ratio) \u2014 insiders sell for many reasons but buy for only one","Healthy payout ratio leaves room for both dividend growth and reinvestment"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"(JMSB) Price Dynamics and Execution-Aware Positioning","source":"news.stocktradersdaily.com","link":"https://news.stocktradersdaily.com/news_release/81/JMSB_Price_Dynamics_and_Execution-Aware_Positioning_111226043202_1794475922.html","date":"2026-11-12","timestamp":1794475922.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.494,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"John Marshall Bancorp (NASDAQ:JMSB) Stock Moved Down to \u201cHold\u201d by Wall Street Zen","source":"thelincolnianonline.com","link":"https://www.thelincolnianonline.com/2026/11/08/john-marshall-bancorp-nasdaqjmsb-stock-moved-down-to-hold-by-wall-street-zen.html","date":"2026-11-08","timestamp":1794130651.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.649,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"John Marshall Bancorp (NASDAQ:JMSB) Stock Bumped Up to Hold by Zacks Research","source":"thelincolnianonline.com","link":"https://www.thelincolnianonline.com/2026/11/04/john-marshall-bancorp-nasdaqjmsb-stock-bumped-up-to-hold-by-zacks-research.html","date":"2026-11-04","timestamp":1793797650.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.557,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Trading Systems Reacting to (JMSB) Volatility","source":"news.stocktradersdaily.com","link":"https://news.stocktradersdaily.com/news_release/91/Trading_Systems_Reacting_to_JMSB_Volatility_110226043402_1793612042.html","date":"2026-11-02","timestamp":1793612042.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.0,"tier":0,"scope":"company